WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.125
What has to move.

2
00:00:01.125 --> 00:00:06.850
Chapters 02 through 04 make two points
that drive everything in this session.

3
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.462
First, placement becomes attributes.

4
00:00:09.462 --> 00:00:12.972
In Active Directory, an object's
position in the forest,

5
00:00:12.972 --> 00:00:17.963
domain and OU tree decides who administers
it and which Group Policy applies.

6
00:00:17.963 --> 00:00:21.162
Move a user to another OU
and its administrators,

7
00:00:21.162 --> 00:00:26.762
inherited policies and often its access
all change without anyone editing the user.

8
00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:28.587
Entra ID has no tree.

9
00:00:28.587 --> 00:00:31.757
It governs objects by what
they carry: attributes,

10
00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:35.237
memberships, role assignments
and policy conditions.

11
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:40.087
Two limits decide how far the usual
answer, administrative units, gets you.

12
00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:45.287
Administrative units can't nest, so an
OU tree becomes a flat set of units.

13
00:00:45.287 --> 00:00:47.926
An object can belong to
at most thirty of them.

14
00:00:47.926 --> 00:00:52.384
And for synced users, Graph treats
the extension attributes as read-only,

15
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:56.175
because their source of authority
is still Active Directory.

16
00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:58.798
So the values your rules
match on are written

17
00:00:58.798 --> 00:01:01.350
on-premises and arrive
with each sync cycle.

18
00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.775
Second, the surface is bigger
than users and groups.

19
00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:08.475
The program's inventory names
eleven dependency types.

20
00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:11.013
The first five are what
plans usually count.

21
00:01:11.013 --> 00:01:17.624
Types six through nine, DNS and DHCP, PKI,
RADIUS and the Network Policy Server,

22
00:01:17.624 --> 00:01:22.976
and LDAP-bound applications,
fail silently when AD goes away.

23
00:01:22.976 --> 00:01:27.199
Types ten and eleven, service
principal names and trusts,

24
00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:30.075
have no direct counterpart in Entra ID.

25
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:32.137
Each dependency gets an adapter.

26
00:01:32.137 --> 00:01:38.076
The PKI, RADIUS and LDAP proxy adapters
are critical, because they fail silently.

27
00:01:38.076 --> 00:01:39.931
The sync engine finishes last,

28
00:01:39.931 --> 00:01:44.387
because it's what keeps AD in production
until everything else has moved.

29
00:01:44.387 --> 00:01:49.060
The rest of this session is how you find
all of that, rebuild it, and prove it.
